    "q-184073": {
      "dom_id": "step-q184073",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=2{q}^{8}+14{q}^{4}-4,$ where $q\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =2{q}^{8}+14{q}^{4}-4 \\\\  & =2({{field-2}}-2) \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}} is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNotice that all terms in the expression $2{q}^{8}+14{q}^{4}-4$ have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{even}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =2{q}^{8}+14{q}^{4}-4 \\\\  & =2({{{q}^{8}+7{q}^{4}-2}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a={q}^{8}+7{q}^{4}-2$ is an integer.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
